WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
 And the idea with Live Healthy Chicago is

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.170
 really to make a structure that is

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.080
 flexible,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.080
 right?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.390
 One of the challenges with public health

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.840
 is that it is inherently inflexible, that

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.240
 funding

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.800
 is specific.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.560
 I'm going to fund HIV, not everything

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.990
 that goes up until the point that a

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.080
 person might

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.680
 contract HIV, or not everything after and

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.840
 around their illness, but just the

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:27.440
 disease,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.160
 just those individuals.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.480
 And we know that a person is part of a

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.890
 larger community, is part of their

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.120
 neighborhood,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.800
 is part of a family.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.090
 And so if we can pull out what is very

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.840
 specific funding and make a model that is

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.680
 nimble to

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.630
 needs, well, yes, we're talking about

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.710
 hypertension today, this doesn't need to

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:48.600
 be the thing that

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.600
 we talk about forever.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.700
 And if we do this well, not only do we

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.100
 have a structure to address what we know

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:54.920
 are historic

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.500
 inequities, but we also have a structure

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.320
 that's nimble for the thing I don't know,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:00.440
 right?

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.280
 For the next crisis, because no one could

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.000
 have predicted COVID before COVID.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.580
 And no one can predict what our next

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.040
 public health crisis will be.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.160
 But if we're all sitting at the table

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.350
 together, we can speed up our city's

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.600
 ability to respond

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.800
 and respond thoughtfully, that centers

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.870
 community in a way that maybe

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.560
 historically hasn't been

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.000
 seen, especially here in Chicago.

